        <p class="profile-detail-region">City · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ctesiphon</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 125 BCE – c. 800 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the late 120s BCE, on the site of a military camp that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a> had set up across the river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, about 35 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, at the place now called Al-Mada'in.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Royal capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>; the metropolis called al-Mada'in, 'The Cities'; Roman attacks of the 2nd and 3rd centuries; seat of the patriarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>; the Arab conquest of 637; the Taq Kasra</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ctesiphon is an archaeological site near the Iraqi town of Salman Pak, where the Taq Kasra, sometimes called the Arch of Ctesiphon, is the most conspicuous structure remaining.</span></div>
