    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ctesiphon began in the late 120s BCE as a Parthian military camp across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>, became the capital around 58 BCE and merged with Seleucia and nearby towns into a metropolis.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took it several times, three times in the 2nd century alone, and in 226 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> made it their capital and enlarged it.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> occupied it in early 637, and after the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in the 760s it declined and was depopulated by the end of the 8th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the late 6th and early 7th century Ctesiphon was listed as the largest city in the world by some accounts, and it measured 30 square kilometres, more than twice the 13.7 square kilometres of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 4th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

