    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">late 120s BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Ctesiphon is founded on the site of a Parthian military camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 58 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The city becomes the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> under Orodes II</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">116</span><span class="rf-text">The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> captures Ctesiphon, and Hadrian returns it in 117</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">197</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> sacks the city and sells thousands of its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">226</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> hold Ctesiphon and make it their capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">541</span><span class="rf-text">After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> builds a new city near Ctesiphon for the people he captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">637</span><span class="rf-text">The Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> occupy Ctesiphon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">760s</span><span class="rf-text">The founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> draws away the city's political and economic life</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia.jpg" alt="Mithridates I of Parthia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Mithradates_I_of_Parthia,_Seleucia_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ctesiphon stood on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, across the river from the Hellenistic city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>. It was founded in the late 120s BCE, built on the site of a military camp established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-parthia">Mithridates I of Parthia</a>. In the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>'s account it was a large village, which the Parthian kings used as their winter residence so that the people of Seleucia would not be oppressed by having Scythian soldiery quartered among them. Strabo wrote that, because of Parthian power, Ctesiphon had become a city rather than a village, equipped with buildings and with wares and arts that pleased the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. The kings spent the winter there for the healthy air and summered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a>.</p><p>The reign of Gotarzes I saw Ctesiphon reach a peak as a political and commercial centre, and the city became the capital of the empire around 58 BCE under Orodes II. The move west may have been due in part to the nearness of the earlier capitals, Mithradatkirt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatompylos">Hecatompylos</a> in Hyrcania, to Scythian incursions. Gradually the city merged with Seleucia and other nearby settlements to form a cosmopolitan metropolis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="Khosrow I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_the_Sasanian_king_Khosrow_I_Anushirvan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ctesiphon was greatly enlarged and flourished under Sasanian rule, which began by 226, and it turned into a metropolis known in Arabic as al-Mada'in and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> as Mahoze. It developed into a rich commercial city, merging with the towns along both shores of the river, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>. Ctesiphon and its surroundings were therefore sometimes called 'The Cities'. The city measured 30 square kilometres, more than twice the 13.7 square kilometres of imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 4th century, and in the late 6th and early 7th century some accounts listed it as the largest city in the world.</p><p>After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 541, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> built a new city near Ctesiphon for the inhabitants he captured and called it Veh-Antiok-Xusrō, which means 'village of Antioch Khosrow built this'. Local people called it Rumagan, 'town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>', and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> al-Rumiyya. After a campaign in 573, John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> wrote that no fewer than 292,000 people had been deported from Dara, Apamia and other Syrian towns to the new city, and later cited a letter giving no more than 30,000 prisoners. The first number is thought not to be meant literally. Khosrow built a number of other fortified cities along with it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajan.jpg" alt="Trajan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traianus_Glyptothek_Munich_72.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ctesiphon belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> from its founding, and the Parthian kings made it their capital around 58 BCE. By 226 it was in the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, which also made it its capital and ended the Parthian dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. For over eight hundred years it served as a royal capital of the Iranian empires.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took the city four or five times in its history, three times in the 2nd century alone. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> captured it in 116, and his successor Hadrian willingly returned it in 117 as part of a peace settlement. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avidius-cassius">Avidius Cassius</a> captured it in 164 during another Parthian war but abandoned it when peace was concluded. In 197 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> sacked it. Under Sasanian rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a> advanced on Ctesiphon in 233 and, as Herodian corroborates, his armies suffered a humiliating defeat against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>. The emperor Carus sacked the city uncontested in 283, during a period of civil upheaval. Some historians have claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> captured it, but no source states this explicitly and scholars still debate it.</p><p>In 590 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a> of the House of Mihran drove the newly ascended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and a year later Khosrow, with Byzantine aid, reconquered his lands. In 627 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> surrounded the city and left after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> accepted his peace terms. In 629 the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> held it briefly before his assassination, and the Pahlav and Parsig factions of the empire then fought constantly.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city's population under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> was heavily mixed and included Arameans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>. Several religions were practised there, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. Church councils of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> met at the city or at the Greek colony across the river in about 325 and again in 410, and in 497 the first East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> patriarch, Mar Babai I, fixed his see at Seleucia-Ctesiphon. Manichaeans, a dualist church, continued to be mentioned there under Umayyad rule and kept a patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the city until the surviving Manichaeans fled in the ninth century and moved it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>The city was divided into districts. The oldest inhabited part lay on the eastern side, which Arabic sources call the Old City and where the Sasanian residence, the White Palace, stood. The southern side, Asbānbar, was known for its halls, riches, games, stables and baths, and the Taq Kasra stood there. The western side was Veh-Ardashir, 'the good city of Ardashir', which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> called Mahoza, Christians Kokhe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> Behrasir. It was populated by many wealthy Jews and was the seat of the Nestorian patriarch's church. Further districts included Valashabad, Hanbu Shapur, Darzanidan, Veh Jondiu-Khosrow, Nawinabad and Kardakadh.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septimius-severus.jpg" alt="Septimius Severus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Septimius_Severus_busto-Musei_Capitolini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ctesiphon's strategic importance made it a military objective of the Roman emperors in their eastern wars, and it was sacked more than once. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> sacked it in 197 and carried off thousands of its inhabitants, whom he sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Carus sacked it in 283. The Battle of Ctesiphon was fought in 363.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> some of the fame of al-Mada'in faded because of the popularity of his new winter residence, Dastagerd. In 628 a deadly plague struck Ctesiphon, al-Mada'in and the rest of the western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, and it killed Khosrow's son and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>.</p><p>In the mid-630s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> defeated the Sasanians at the Battle of al-Qādisiyyah and then attacked Ctesiphon, which they occupied in early 637. Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas quickly seized Valashabad and made a treaty with the people of Veh-Antiok-Xusrō and Veh-Ardashir: those of the former could leave or acknowledge Muslim authority and pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>. The Muslims found Ctesiphon completely desolated, because the Sasanian royal family, nobles and troops had fled. They took some troops captive and seized many riches from the royal treasury, and the throne hall of the Taq Kasra was briefly used as a mosque. Much of the population fled after the capture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights.jpg" alt="One Thousand and One Nights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sughrat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Al-Mubashshir ibn Fatik, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Political and economic fortune passed elsewhere, and the city went into rapid decline, especially after the Abbasid capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was founded in the 760s. Ctesiphon soon became a ghost town and was depopulated by the end of the 8th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur took much of the material needed to build Baghdad from its ruins. He also tried to demolish the palace and reuse its bricks for his own palace but gave up when the undertaking proved too vast. He used the town of al-Rumiya as the Abbasid capital for a few months. Ctesiphon is believed to be the basis for the city of Isbanir in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights</a>.</p><p>In November 1915 the ruins were the site of a major battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> defeated British troops attempting to capture Baghdad and drove them back some 40 miles before trapping the force and compelling it to surrender.</p><p>A German Oriental Society expedition under Oscar Reuther excavated in 1928 and 1929, and a joint expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> State Museums and the Metropolitan Museum of Art worked there in 1931 and 1932. An Italian team from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a> worked in the late 1960s and early 1970s on the far side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, at Veh Ardashir. The Taq Kasra, once part of the royal palace, is estimated to date between the 3rd and 6th centuries and stands in the Iraqi town of Salman Pak.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ctesiphon, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> south-east of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, was founded in the late 120s BCE and served as the royal capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> occupied it in 637, and it was depopulated by the end of the 8th century after Baghdad was founded.</em></p>

